D cause GAME_OVER.\nSo the pattern seems to be:\n- Top row green blocks (row 27): all can be clicked safely\n- Bottom row green blocks (row 33): only some can be clicked safely\n- Specifically, (33, 15) caused GAME_OVER, but (33, 33) was safe\nThe purple targets are at columns 15, 21, 27. The bottom row green blocks at columns 15, 21, 27 are above the purple targets (or aligned with them). The bottom row green blocks at columns 33, 39, 45, 51 are NOT aligned with the purple targets.\nSo maybe the rule is:\n- Can click on top row green blocks (any column)\n- Can click on bottom row green blocks that are NOT aligned with purple targets (columns 33, 39, 45, 51)\n- Cannot click on bottom row green blocks that ARE aligned with purple targets (columns 15, 21, 27)\nLet me test this hypothesis by clicking on the remaining bottom row green blocks that are NOT aligned with purple targets (columns 39, 45, 51).", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# Continue removing bottom row green blocks NOT aligned with purple targets\n# Purple targets are at columns 15, 21, 27\n# Bottom row green blocks at columns 39, 45, 51 are NOT aligned with purple targets\n\nfor col in [39, 45, 51]:\n    result = action([{'action': 'MOUSE', 'row': 33, 'col': col}])\n    print(f\"Clicked (33, {col}): state={result.get('state')}, game_over={result.get('game_over')}, level_completed={result.get('level_completed')}\")\n    if result.get('game_over') or result.get('level_completed'):\n        break\n\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\ngreens = [n for n in seg['nodes'] if n['color'] == 'N']\nprint(f\"Green blocks remaining: {len(greens)}\")\nfor g in greens:\n    center_r = sum(p[0] for p in g['boundary']) // len(g['boundary'])\n    center_c = sum(p[1] for p in g['boundary']) // len(g['boundary'])\n    print(f\"  At ({center_r}, {center_c})\")\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inCont